Draw the **Lattice transport belt** in both its forms, as a 16-frame **sprite
sheet** of an animated factory conveyor, using only the drawing tool.

The belt is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a grid-based factory simulation
drawn flat and top-down; the belt is its ground-level layer, drawn as a flat 2D
surface seen from straight above. You are drawing **two forms of the
same belt**:

- **Frames 0–7 — the straight belt**, one tile of conveyor **flowing to the right
  (East)**.
- **Frames 8–15 — the curve**, one corner tile where that same belt turns 90
  degrees: flow **enters at the West edge travelling East** and **leaves at the
  South edge travelling South**, curving clockwise.

Each form is a dark **single** continuous metal surface — one belt whose full
width carries two items side by side, not two belts — with rails along its edges,
tread blades patterned across the surface, and a central row of amber chevrons
painted on it. In each form the **whole surface — blades and chevrons together —
scrolls downstream** across its eight frames, so the belt itself reads as moving
and each loop is seamless. The renderer rotates and mirrors these two sprites for
every other direction and corner, so you draw only the East-flowing straight belt
and only the West-in / South-out curve.

**The two forms must match.** They are one belt, so a straight tile feeding a
corner has to read as a single conveyor changing direction rather than two
different belts meeting at a seam. That means building both from one construction
language (the same palette, the same rails, the same blade and chevron pitch),
making the curve's mouths butt flush against a straight tile edge for edge, and
scrolling both in phase so the pattern flows through the junction without a jump.
The brief specifies exactly what each of those requires; it is the substance of
this case, not a finishing touch.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a frame
with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a
per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 0 --y 0 --width 32 --height 32 --color "#34383d"
draw-sheet fill-circle --frame 8 --cx 0 --cy 31 --r 31 --color "#6b7178"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the sprite sheet up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png`
matches the brief: eight frames of a straight East-flowing belt tile that tiles
horizontally, eight frames of a 90-degree corner tile sweeping from a full-width
West entry face round to a full-width South exit face, both built from the same
palette, rails, tread blades and chevrons, both scrolling their whole surface
downstream by the same step each frame so each loop closes seamlessly, and the two
meeting flush and in phase so a belt feeding the corner flows through it. When you
are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished
submission.
